-- title:
--   A universal cyclotomic discrete valuation ring over Z₀
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime with $3 \le q$, and let $Z_0$ be a commutative domain that is a discrete valuation ring whose maximal ideal is generated by the image of $q$, i.e. $\mathfrak m_{Z_0} = (q)$. The assertion is the existence of a type $V$ carrying a commutative ring structure, which is a domain and a discrete valuation ring, together with a $Z_0$-algebra structure making $V$ a finite and free $Z_0$-module, and with the property that the residue field of $V$ is finite whenever that of $Z_0$ is; furthermore there exist an element $\varpi \in V$ generating the maximal ideal of $V$ and generating $V$ as a $Z_0$-algebra, in the sense that $\mathfrak m_V = (\varpi)$ and $\mathrm{adjoin}_{Z_0}\{\varpi\} = V$, and a unit $\varepsilon \in V$ with $\varpi^{q-1} = \varepsilon\, q$ in $V$ and $\sum_{i < q} (1-\varpi)^i = 0$. Finally, this datum is universal for $q$-th roots of unity in the cyclotomic sense: for every commutative ring $R$ with a $Z_0$-algebra structure and every $\zeta \in R$ satisfying $\sum_{i < q} \zeta^i = 0$, there is a $Z_0$-algebra homomorphism $\iota : V \to R$ with $\iota(\varpi) = 1 - \zeta$.
--
--   This constructs the cyclotomic extension $Z_0[\zeta_q] = Z_0[X]/(\Phi_q(X+1))$ of a discrete valuation ring with uniformiser $q$, presented as a totally ramified discrete valuation ring generated by its uniformiser $\varpi = 1 - \zeta_q$, together with the universal property that pins down maps out of it by the image of $\varpi$; the relation $\varpi^{q-1} = \varepsilon q$ records the ramification. It is used in the analysis of completions of modular curves at the cusps and in the associated unramifiedness statements, where a coefficient ring containing a $q$-th root of unity and generated by the corresponding uniformiser is required; the Eisenstein input is supplied by the regularity of $\mathrm{AdjoinRoot}$ of a monic polynomial whose lower coefficients lie in the maximal ideal and whose constant term avoids its square, together with the fact that regular local rings are domains.
